            "title": "Increased U.S. trade deficit in 2017 illustrates dangers of ignoring ...",
            "snippets": [
              "## Increased U.S. trade deficit in 2017 illustrates dangers of ignoring the overvalued dollar\nThe U.S. trade deficit with China reached a new record of $375.2 billion in 2017, up from $347 billion in 2016, an increase of $28.2 billion (8.1 percent). China is a particular source of concern in trade in steel and aluminum, industries in which that country has accumulated massive amounts of subsidized, and often state-owned excess production capacity, over the past two decades",
              "The most important cause of large and growing U.S. trade deficits is persistent currency undervaluation by countries such as China, Japan, and Korea, which have run large, persistent trade surpluses, as well as large structural surpluses accumulated by Germany. The real, trade-weighted value of the U.S. dollar increased sharply in 2014 and 2015, as shown below"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.forbes.com/sites/salvatorebabones/2018/01/12/the-u-s-trade-deficit-with-china-grew-in-2017-because-the-u-s-economy-is-booming/",
            "title": "The U.S. Economy Is Booming, Which Is Why The U.S. Trade Deficit ...",
            "snippets": [
              "America's trade deficit with China was $288 billion in 2017, according to Chinese data. If you think that's big, just wait until the U.S. reports its official figures on February 6.\nAsia",
              "# The U.S. Economy Is Booming, Which Is Why The U.S. Trade Deficit With China Grew In 2017\nChina's trade surplus with the United States rose by 13% in 2017 to a record $288 billion, according to Chinese official data. The actual figure reported on the Chinese side was 1.87 trillion yuan, which most Western news outlets converted to $276 billion.",
              "But not big enough. The U.S. reports trade figures monthly, and U.S. data showed that the trade deficit was already $342 billion by the end of November. Consolidating the $30 billion U.S. surplus with Hong Kong (most of which goes to China) puts the all-China deficit at $312 billion for the first 11 months of 2017",
              "Add in another $30 billion or so for December, and the full-year 2017 U.S. trade deficit with China and Hong Kong is likely to come in at around $340 billion",
              "That means that the final 2017 U.S. deficit with China/HKG may be up 17%-18% from the $280 billion consolidated China/HKG deficit recorded for 2016.  The official U.S. trade deficit with China reported by the U.S. Census Bureau on February 6 will be big, growing and politically messy."
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.politico.com/story/2017/12/05/us-trade-deficit-china-280949",
            "title": "U.S. trade gap soars as imports from China hit record high - POLITICO",
            "snippets": [
              "# U.S. trade gap soars as imports from China hit record high\nDonald Trump is pictured here. | AP Photo\n\nThe monthly trade gap totaled $48.7 billion, the highest level for a full month since President Donald Trump took office on Jan.",
              "The U.S. trade deficit jumped 8.6 percent in October as imports from China and other suppliers hit a record high ahead of the holiday shopping season, a Commerce Department report released Tuesday showed.\n\nThe monthly trade gap totaled $48.7 billion, the highest level for a full month since President Donald Trump took office on Jan.",
              "Ahead of the election, a Trump campaign adviser asserted they could eliminate the deficit in one or two years. Instead, the 2017 deficit is on track to exceed the 2016 level of $505 billion",
              "Imports of goods and services hit a record high of $244.6 billion as the U.S. economy continued to strengthen and suck in more goods from abroad. Imports totaled $48.2 billion from China, $39.4 billion from the EU and $28.7 billion from Mexico — all record highs",
              "In addition, the average price for imported crude oil was $47.26 per barrel during October, the highest since August 2015, a Commerce Department highlights sheet showed.\n\nU.S. exports totaled $195.9 billion in October, unchanged from September. However, exports to China hit $13 billion, the highest since December 2013"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.usnews.com/news/economy/articles/2017-07-06/us-trade-deficit-with-china-widens-as-north-korea-showdown-looms",
            "title": "U.S. Trade Deficit With China Widens as North Korea Showdown Looms",
            "snippets": [
              "# U.S. Trade Deficit With China Widens as North Korea Showdown Looms\nBillions of dollars in trade hang in the balance as the U.S. reportedly weighs retaliation against China for doing business with North Korea.",
              "America's trade deficit contracted slightly in May but was still considerably larger through the first five months of 2017 than it was a year prior",
              "But as trade officials under President Donald Trump gear up for a possible showdown with China over Chinese-North Korean commerce, the latest export and import data show a significant share of U.S. trade potentially hangs in the balance",
              "### ADP: U.S. Job Growth Slows to 158K\nExports through the first five months of the year clocked in at nearly $958 billion, up more than $54 billion from the same period a year ago. Imports, meanwhile, hit nearly $1.2 trillion, an increase of more than $81 billion",
              "\"Of note, the trade deficit with key trading partners, including Canada, Mexico and China, are all wider year-to-date compared to the same period in 2016 – a development which will likely heighten the protectionist rhetoric coming from Washington,\" a team of researchers at Oxford Economics wrote in a research note Thursday",
              "Trade between China and North Korea grew almost 40% in the first quarter.",
              "At issue for Trump is a trade report published this week that suggests Chinese trade with North Korea was up 15 percent during the first five months of the year – though that increase was driven largely by North Korea buying Chinese products. Chinese purchases of North Korean exports appeared to have cooled",
              "### With Job Creation Slowing, Who's Hiring?\nFor years, China has been the single largest importer of products into the U.S. – and one of its most lopsided trading partners. America's trade deficit with China in goods and services through the first quarter of the year rose to nearly $82 billion – a larger value than America's deficits with Germany, Japan, Mexico, India, Italy and Taiwan over that period combined",
              "In the first three months of 2017 alone, China imported more than $127 billion of goods and services into the U.S",
              "And yet China is also one of America's most significant export markets. Though the Chinese sell significantly more products to the U.S. than they buy, China was still America's third-largest export market in the first quarter, behind only Canada and Mexico. China bought up more than $45 billion of American products in the first quarter and has increased their purchases in every quarter since the beginning of last year",
              "### Report: Bannon Pushing for Tax Hike on Wealthy\nSeveral members of Trump's Cabinet reportedly expressed opposition to the plan, but it is as of yet unclear whether the president will follow through. The ultimate concern is that actions against China would lead to higher prices for consumers and reduced international selling options for American producers.\n\nThose concerns are mitigated for some, though, by the sheer size of America's trade deficit with China and the fact that commerce pressures between the two countries have been bubbling for years",
              "\"I think over time, there inevitably is going to be significant conflict [between the U.S. and China]. If you look at U.S. trade relations around the world, China does stand out. We have by far our biggest trade deficit with China,\" Harris said. \"This is an area where actually there's common agreement among economists and policymakers in the U.S"
            ]
          },
